	(footprint "electra:Conn_MicroJST1.25-SHstyle_1x02-1MP_P1.25mm_Vertical_SMD"
		(layer "F.Cu")
		(uuid "5ede6e0a-f772-49ed-aaa3-af1a3e5c3f8f")
		(at 101.012 61.2724 -90)
		(descr "2-way 1.25 mm vertical (top-entry) SMD wire-to-board header with two anchor pads, for the unbranded part sold as '10sets MICRO JST 1.25 ... Pitch Vertical / Horizontal SMD Pin Header + Housing + Terminal'. Built from the vendor drawing in docs/datasheets/JST 1.25.pdf. This is NOT JST GH and must not be given the GH footprint: the vendor's own table makes the 2-way body far smaller than GH, and the way it scales identifies the family outright - the body is always the pin span plus 3.0 mm, which is JST SH's proportion, where genuine GH adds 4.5 mm and Molex PicoBlade 6.4 mm. So the part is an SH-shaped connector stretched to a 1.25 mm pitch, i.e. the connector usually sold for LiPo balance leads, and it should not be expected to intermate with either genuine family. Land pattern: signal pads 0.50 x 1.60 mm on the 1.25 mm pitch, so at x = +/-0.625; anchor pads 2.00 x 3.00 mm centred at x = +/-1.60 on the opposite side of the part, with a 0.60 mm gap between the two pad bands. The whole pattern is centred on the origin, which is how KiCad's own JST SH and GH vertical footprints are datumed, and it puts the signal row at y = +1.80 and the anchor row at y = -1.10 - values that sit neatly between stock SH (+1.325 / -1.200) and stock GH (+1.950 / -1.400). Two numbers here are reasoned rather than stated, and both want confirming with calipers before fab. The vendor letters its table A/B/C and then brackets its drawings and photo inconsistently with it, so read the arithmetic instead: the A column is exactly (n-1) x 1.25 on all fifteen rows and can only be the pin span, B is that plus 3.0 and C that plus 1.95. Taking C = 3.20 as the plastic body puts its ends at x = +/-1.60, and anchor pads centred on those end edges land exactly where they are drawn here; taking B = 4.25 as the overall width including the metal tabs then leaves 0.475 mm of pad proud of the part for a solder fillet, and 1.00 mm of pad tucked under the body where the tab folds beneath. The alternative reading, C as the anchor-pad centre span, independently gives the same +/-1.60, which is the reason to trust it. The second inference is the 0.60 mm figure being the gap between the pad bands rather than some other offset; stock SH leaves 0.85 mm there and stock GH 1.10 mm, so 0.60 is tight but in family. Verify on arrival: anchor-tab centre span 3.20 mm, and the clear gap between the tab band and the pin row 0.60 mm. F.Fab is drawn as the 4.25 x 3.70 mm overall envelope rather than the plastic alone, because the tab outline is not dimensioned; the two inner vertical lines mark x = +/-1.60 where the 3.20 mm plastic body ends and the tabs begin. The mating plug is the housing from the same kit, which is the entire point of using this part - see docs/wiring.md, where the speaker arrangement is chosen so that the only connector that has to mate is one whose halves both come out of this kit. The 3D model is built by pcb/tools/model_jst125.py, which takes the pin count as an argument and covers the 1x03 at J1 from the same numbers; it is drawn rather than downloaded because no vendor supplies a model for an unbranded kit part. Its one important number is the height, 4.75 mm off the sheet's front elevation, which the land pattern never needed: this is top-entry, so the clearance question is the headroom above the board for the plug coming down, not the body itself.")
		(tags "connector jst 1.25 micro sh-style smd vertical wire-to-board")
